  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**What the supply\u2011graph tells us**\n\nUsing the *downstream* traversal (max\u202f2 tiers) from **Xiamen Tungsten**, the model identified more than 100 downstream entities that could be exposed to a shortage of tungsten\u2011based raw materials.  \n\n- **Tier\u202f1 (direct customers or immediate alternative suppliers)** \u2013 13 companies with high confidence, e.g.:  \n  - Contemporary Amperex Technology (CATL) \u2013 EV lithium\u2011ion battery cells  \n  - Sunwoda Electronic \u2013 lithium\u2011ion cells & packs  \n  - Hunan Corun New Energy \u2013 Ni\u2011MH battery cells  \n  - Gotion High\u2011Tech \u2013 LFP battery cells  \n  - Sumitomo Electric Industries \u2013 automotive wiring harnesses  \n  - Murata Manufacturing \u2013 MLCCs (multilayer ceramic capacitors)  \n  - Samsung\u202fSDI \u2013 lithium\u2011ion battery cells  \n  - TDK \u2013 passive electronic components (capacitors, inductors)  \n  - Panasonic \u2013 automotive lithium\u2011ion cells  \n  - BYD \u2013 battery\u2011electric vehicles (BEVs)  \n  - CALB \u2013 EV lithium\u2011ion cells (LFP/NMC)  \n  - Zhejiang Founder Motor \u2013 EV drive/traction motors  \n\n- **Tier\u202f2 (customers of the Tier\u202f1 firms, or downstream vehicle manufacturers)** \u2013 a long list of major automakers and component makers, e.g.:  \n  Mitsubishi Motors, ADIENT, Dongfeng Motor, Tokai Rika, Zhejiang Leapmotor Technology, Beijing Automotive, KG\u202fMobility, Tata Motors, Stellantis, Samsung Electronics (auto\u2011electronics), Robert\u202fBosch, Minebea\u202fMitsumi, Aisin, Volvo\u202fAB, China\u202fFAW, Chery Automobile, SAIC\u2011GM\u2011Wuling, Renault, FORVIA, Toyota Motor, Hyundai Motor, Nissan Motor, General Motors, Volkswagen, BorgWarner, Magna International, Ford Motor, etc. (all flagged as \u201chigh\u201d or \u201cmedium\u201d confidence by the graph).\n\nThese companies are **structurally at risk** because they either use tungsten\u2011based raw materials directly (e.g., MLCCs, battery cathodes) or rely on Tier\u202f1 firms that do.\n\n---\n\n**Evidence gathered**\n\n| Company | Tool(s) consulted | What was found |\n|---------|-------------------|----------------|\n| **Samsung\u202fSDI** | `get_supplier_info` (no filing) | No SEC filing exists for the requested date; therefore no direct mention of Xiamen Tungsten or any supply\u2011disruption. |\n| **TDK** | `get_supplier_info` (2008 20\u2011F) | The filing is a standard annual report that discusses raw\u2011material sourcing in general terms but contains **no specific reference to Xiamen Tungsten, tungsten supplies, or any disruption affecting its operations**. |\n| **All other Tier\u202f1 & Tier\u202f2 firms** | `search_corpus` (global query \u201cXiamen Tungsten\u201d) | The search returned only unrelated passages (e.g., TDK subsidiary listings, aluminum\u2011company agreements). **No filing or news item mentions Xiamen Tungsten, a disruption in 2010\u201109, or any impact on these downstream companies**. |\n| **Reachable but not enriched list** (e.g., \u201crechi precision\u201d, \u201cjiangxi xiushui \u2026\u201d) | Not examined \u2013 they were flagged as reachable only, with no enrichment confidence; no tool calls left to investigate them. |\n\n---\n\n**What could NOT be confirmed**\n\n* **Direct impact** \u2013 None of the filings or corpus mentions that any of the identified downstream companies experienced a material shortage, production slowdown, or other operational issue linked to the September\u202f2010 disruption at Xiamen Tungsten.\n* **Supply\u2011chain adjustments** \u2013 No evidence was found of alternative sourcing agreements, contract renegotiations, or price spikes that would signal a reaction to a tungsten supply shock.\n* **Tier\u202f2 companies\u2019 exposure** \u2013 While the graph shows they are two steps removed from Xiamen Tungsten, we have no documentary proof (SEC filings, press releases, etc.) that they felt any effect.\n\n---\n\n### Bottom\u2011line assessment\n\n1. **Structural risk exists** \u2013 The supply\u2011graph clearly maps a dense network of battery manufacturers, capacitor makers, and automotive OEMs that depend\u2014directly or indirectly\u2014on tungsten\u2011based inputs supplied by Xiamen Tungsten.\n2. **No corroborating evidence found** \u2013 Within the limited tool budget (four calls) we were unable to locate any SEC filing, 8\u2011K, 20\u2011F, news item, or other corporate disclosure that explicitly links the September\u202f2010 disruption at Xiamen Tungsten to operational impacts on those downstream firms.\n3. **Investigation incomplete by necessity** \u2013 Because only four tool calls are allowed, many Tier\u202f1/Tier\u202f2 companies (e.g., CATL, Sunwoda, Murata, BYD, major automakers) could not be individually verified through their own filings or targeted corpus searches. The \u201creachable but not enriched\u201d set also remains unexamined.\n\n**Therefore:** the disruption at Xiamen Tungsten **potentially threatens a wide swath of battery\u2011 and automotive\u2011related companies**, but **the publicly available evidence does not confirm that any of these firms actually experienced material effects**. Further research\u2014e.g., targeted searches of each Tier\u202f1 company\u2019s 8\u2011K/10\u2011K filings, news databases, or supplier\u2011relationship disclosures\u2014would be required to move from \u201cstructurally at risk\u201d to \u201cconfirmed impacted\u201d.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (104) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - contemporary amperex technology: no evidence gathered\n  - sunwoda electronic: no evidence gathered\n  - hunan corun new energy: no evidence gathered\n  - gotion high-tech: no evidence gathered\n  - sumitomo electric industries: no evidence gathered\n  - murata manufacturing: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung sdi: no evidence gathered\n  - tdk: only stale evidence (886 days old)\n  - byd